-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+## Eviction Policy
+
+The cache uses 2Q eviction. Two resident queues separate sheets seen once from sheets editors return to:
+
+- Probation (A1) is a FIFO queue for sheets seen once. Its target size is 3 GiB, one quarter of the cache.
+- Protected (Am) is an LRU queue for sheets whose reuse has been demonstrated. It may use the remaining capacity.
+
+The worker also keeps a bounded, key-only probation history (A1out) recording recent probation evictions. The history stores no JPEG data and defaults to 8,192 keys.
+
+A completed sheet whose key is neither resident nor in probation history enters probation. A request that finds a key resident in probation promotes it to the most-recent end of protected. A request that finds a key in probation history but not resident admits the completed sheet directly to the most-recent end of protected. A request for a resident protected sheet refreshes its recency. Only requests that find a sheet resident in probation or its history count as reuse; pending-render retries for a key still being rendered do not promote it.
+
+When admission would exceed 12 GiB, the worker evicts the oldest probation entries until probation is at or below its 3 GiB target or the cache fits, then evicts least-recently-used protected entries if the cache still exceeds 12 GiB. A probation eviction adds the key to the front of probation history; entries falling out of the bounded history are forgotten. Protected evictions do not enter history. Queue contents and history are worker-local and are not exposed through the preview interface.
+
+The 3 GiB probation target and 8,192-key history bound are deployment parameters, not part of the external contract.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Cache eviction policy | 2Q: FIFO probation, LRU protected, key-only probation history | LRU; frequency-based eviction with aging | Long screenings create many one-use sheets while comparison sessions revisit a small working set. 2Q sends first-use sheets through probation while a second request promotes a sheet to protected, so a sequential scan does not evict comparison sheets. LRU follows recent work cheaply but a long scan displaces the comparison set. Aged frequency counts retain repeatedly popular clips but require counter maintenance, add aging work, and can keep a previous project's favorites after a switch. |
+| Probation and protected capacity split | Probation target 3 GiB (25% of the cache); protected may use the remainder | Equal split; larger probation | Reserving most capacity for reused sheets protects the comparison working set, while a 3 GiB probation queue holds roughly 1,500 typical 2 MiB sheets. Once probation reaches its target, a screening scan cycles through probation instead of protected; the target is tunable per production workload. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+1. ✅ Eviction policy: 2Q, chosen to protect comparison sheets during long screenings.
+
+### Deferred
+1. Tune the probation size target and probation-history bound against production workloads.
+2. Decide whether replacement metadata rebuilds from cache contents after a worker restart.
